# Untitled

Nanoarchitectonics with metal-organic frameworks and platinum nanozymes with improved oxygen evolution for enhanced sonodynamic/chemo-therapy
Qian Ren a,1, Nuo Yu a,1, Leyi Wang a, Mei Wen a, Peng Geng a, Qin Jiang a,∗, Maoquan Li b,∗, Zhigang Chen a,∗
a State Key Laboratory for Modification of Chemical Fibers and Polymer Materials, College of Materials Science and Engineering, Donghua University, Shanghai 201620, China b Department of Interventional and Vascular Surgery, Shanghai Tenth People's Hospital, Tongji University School of Medicine, Shanghai 200072, China
G R A P H I C A L A B S T R A C T
DOX@PCN-224/Pt nanoparticles (NPs) have been prepared by firstly synthesizing PCN-224 NPs, then decorating Pt nanozymes and phosphatidylcholine on PCN-224 NPs, and finally loading DOX into the lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs. The DOX@PCN-224/Pt system exhibited combined SDT-chemotherapy and achieved high therapeutical efficiency for cancer cells.
## A R T I C L E I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Article history:  Received 15 October 2021  Revised 6 January 2022  Accepted 7 January 2022
Available online 10 January 2022
Keywords:  Sonodynamic therapy  Metal-organic framework  Nanozyme  Tumor hypoxia  Chemotherapy
Sonodynamic therapy (SDT), which fights against cancers by producing cytotoxic singlet oxygen \(\left({}^{1}0_{2}\right)\) under ultrasound irradiation, has shown great potential in cancer treatment due to its noninvasiveness and deep tissue penetration. However, their practical application suffers from some limitations, such as tumor hypoxia and the inefficient generation of \({}^{1}0_{2}\). To solve these problems, we have prepared the nanoarchitectonics based on metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) containing platinum (Pt) nanozymes with improved oxygen evolution for the enhanced sonodynamic/chemo-therapy effects. As a model of MOF, porous coordination network-224 (PCN-224) nanoparticles with the size of \(100\;\mathrm{nm}\) have been prepared through coordinating tetrakis (4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin (TPP) with \(\mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{IV})\) cations during a solvothermal process, and they are then decorated with Pt nanoclusters \((\sim\!1.5\ \mathrm{nm})\) by in-situ reduction. After being surface-modified with the phosphatidylcholine and loading DOX, the DOX@PCN-224/Pt nanoplatforms exhibit a good \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) catalytic activity, \({}^{1}0_{2}\) generation ability, efficient loading \((26.3\%)\) of DOX, and pH-responsive releasing ability. When the DOX@PCN-224/Pt dispersions are intratumorally
injected into mice, they can convert the endogenous \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) into oxygen for alleviating tumor hypoxia. Moreover, the generated oxygen can further enhance the sensitivity of SDT and tumor cells to chemotherapy by down-regulating the expression of hypoxia-inducible factor \(\alpha\), resulting in the enhanced SDT and chemotherapeutic effect. With these merits, the combination of sonodynamic and chemotherapy of DOX@PCN-224/Pt remarkably inhibits the tumor growth compared to chemotherapy or SDT alone. Therefore, the DOX@PCN-224/Pt nanoplatform serves as an efficient nanoarchitectonics for enhanced sonodynamic/chemo-therapy.
