## 1. Introduction
Due to the uncontrolled growth and rapid spread of cancerous cells, cancer remains a major cause of death in the global world [1–3]. To effectively treat cancer, a variety of new treatment modalities have rapidly emerged, including photodynamic therapy (PDT) [4,5], photothermal therapy (PTT) [6,7], sonodynamic therapy (SDT) [8], starvation therapy [9], chemodynamic therapy (CDT) [10] and so on. Among these treatments, SDT has drawn widespread attention because of its well-recognized safety, noninvasiveness, and high tissue penetration depth [11]. Compared to the light-mediated PDT, SDT utilizes ultrasound (US) to activate sonosensitizers that convert the intratumoral oxygen into toxic singlet oxygen \(\left({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)\) to kill tumor cells. The key driving factor of SDT is the development of sonosensitizers with high \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) production capacity. To date, researchers have developed organic sonosensitizers [12,13], inorganic sonosensitizers [14,15], and organic/inorganic hybrid nanomaterials [16,17]. Among these organic/inorganic hybrid nanomaterials, the metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), coordinated by inorganic metal ions/clusters with organic sonosensitizers, have been proven to have great potential in SDT [18,19]. The porous structures of MOFs can effectively facilitate the rapid diffusion of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\). Moreover, MOFs with large surface areas and porous structures (such as Zr-DTBA [20], GdBBDC [21], and UiO-66 [22]) can function as efficient drug-delivery systems, which are beneficial to the combination therapy of tumors. Our group has also developed a series of MOF nanoparticles by integrating metal ions with hematoporphyrin monomethyl ether (HMME) molecules, including Fe-HMME [8], MnHMME [23], and Gd-HMME [24]. These MOF nanoparticles have good biocompatibility, high SDT performance, satisfactory drug delivery and release capacity. Therefore, the rapid development of MOFs-based sonosensitizers provides a great breakthrough for SDT.
Although the MOFs-based sonosensitizers have been demonstrated to possess high SDT performance, their therapeutic efficacy is still limited by the hypoxia tumor microenvironment [25]. To solve the hypoxia problem, two approaches have been reported to increase the intratumoral oxygen concentration. The first is to develop nanomaterials that can load some oxygen-carrying agents (such as perfluorocarbon [26], hemoglobin [27]) directly to tumors. However, the oxygen-carrying capacity is limited. The second is to employ enzymatic catalysts (natural enzymes and nanozymes) to in situ convert endogenous hydrogen peroxide \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)\) to oxygen. In comparison to natural enzymes, the nanozymes show evident advantages including tunable activity, easy preparation, low cost, high stability and strong resistance to harsh environment [28]. Thus, the nanozymes are promising alternatives to the natural enzymes in various applications, such as cancer therapy [29], antibacterial therapy [30,31], and anti-inflammatory therapy [32]. In cancer therapy, the nanozymes might provide a great opportunity for sufficient oxygen supply in the treatment of solid tumors. Till now, several kinds of nanomaterials have been constructed as nanozymes with catalase activity, for instance, metallic nanocomposites [29], carbon nanostructures [33], and nobel metal
nanoparticles [34–39]. Among the noble metal nanoparticles, the platinum (Pt) nanoparticles have been demonstrated to have great potential of mimicking the activity of catalase [36–39]. For example, Liu et al. designed a novel platinum-copper sulfide (Pt-CuS) Janus porous nanospheres containing tetra-(4-aminophenyl) porphyrin (TAPP) molecules, and Pt nanoparticles can convert endogenous \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to oxygen for overcoming tumor hypoxia and augmenting the \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) production [36]. However, it should be pointed out that the Pt nanozymes are often used in PDT, but seldom used in SDT. In particular, the size control of Pt nanozymes growing on MOFs has been less studied in SDT. Considering these fascinating properties, the introduction of Pt on MOFs-based sonosensitizers might be an ideal way to overcome hypoxia and enhance SDT performance.
It is well known that the therapeutic efficacy of SDT depends on US with high penetrative power, and the SDT will be terminated immediately if the US irradiation is turned off. Thus, it is of great interest to combine SDT with other treatments (e.g. chemotherapy) to further improve the overall therapeutic efficacy through prolonging the therapeutic duration and modality. The chemotherapy can produce a long-lasting chemotherapeutic effect [40,41], which largely compensates for the deficiency of SDT. MOFs and organosilica nanoparticles can be used as carriers of chemotherapeutic drugs or sonosensitizers, achieving the excellent combined effect for SDT and chemotherapy [8,12]. For example, our group integrated \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) ions with HMME molecules to form amorphous metal-organic coordination particles (FeCPs) as sonosensitizer and DOX carrier [8]. The synthesized DOX@FeCPs can achieve efficient SDT-chemotherapy combination therapy for deep tumors. However, recent studies have shown that the hypoxia tumors are highly overexpressed by hypoxia-inducible factor \(\alpha\) (HIF-1\(\alpha\)) [42], which causes the overexpression of drug efflux transporter P-glycoprotein in tumors [43]. This leads to an elevated efflux of drugs from tumor cells, resulting in less effect of chemotherapy. Therefore, it is quite imperative to solve the hypoxia in tumor microenvironment for chemotherapy. It should be noted that there are few studies about combining SDT, chemotherapy and nanozyme for the treatment of hypoxia tumor.
As a typical MOF, porous coordination network-224 (PCN-224) nanoparticles (NPs) are composed of zirconium clusters and tetrakis (4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin (TCPP), and they can serve as a photosensitizer/sonosensitizer to produce \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\). In order to improve their therapeutic effect, herein PCN-224 NPs were used as a model to in-situ grow Pt nanoclusters, followed by phosphatidylcholine coating and chemotherapeutic drugs doxorubicin (DOX) loading. The resultant DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs exhibited good \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) generation ability, high hydrogen peroxide catalytic activity, efficient loading (26.3%) of DOX. When the DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs were intratumorally injected into mice, the DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs exhibited the enhanced \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) generation and DOX efficacy by the decorated Pt nanozymes decomposing excess \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) into \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\). Importantly, the tumor growth inhibition (\(\sim\!84.4\%\)) of DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs upon US irradiation is significantly higher than that in chemotherapy group (DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs) or SDT group (PCN-224/Pt NPs + US), due to the combined sonodynamic/chemo-therapy.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
2.1. MaterialsTetrakis (4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin (TCPP, \(\geq97\%\) ) was obtained from Shanghai Yishi Chemical Co., Ltd. Zirconyl chloride octahydrate \(\mathrm{(ZrOCl_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}\) , \(\geq99.5\%\) ), N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, \(99.7\%\) ), chloroplatinic acid \(\mathrm{(H_{2}P t C l_{6}}\) , \(\geq99.5\%\) ), 1,3-diphenyl isobenzofuran (DPBF, \(97\%\) ), hydrogen peroxide \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) , \(30\%\) , GR), doxorubicin hydrochloride (DOX, \(98\%\) ), benzoic acid and sodium borohydride \(\mathrm{(NaBH_{4}}\) , \(\geq96\%\) ) were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Phosphatidylcholine (PC, \(\geq90\%\) ) was obtained from Shanghai Aladdin Bio Chem Technology Co., Ltd. Calcein acetoxymethyl ester (Calcein-AM), propidium iodide (PI), \(2^{\prime},7^{\prime}\) -dichlorofluorescin diacetate (DCFH-DA), \(^{4^{\prime},6}\) -diamidino-2-phe nylindole dihydrochloride (DAPI), cell counting kit-8 (CCK-8), hematoxylin and eosin (H&E), saline solution were obtained from Beyotime Biotechnology. Mouse colon cancer cell line (CT26), human ovarian cancer cells (SKOV3) and human umbilical vein endothelial cell line (HUVEC) were brought from Type Culture Collection of the Chinese Academy of Sciences, Shanghai, China.
## 2.2. Synthesis of PCN-224
2.2. Synthesis of PCN-224PCN-224 NPs were prepared according to the previous method with slight modifications [44]. Briefly, \(30\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{ZrOCl_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}\) , \(10~\mathrm{mg}\) TCPP and \(280~\mathrm{mg}\) benzoic acid were dissolved in \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) DMF and ultrasonicated for \(15\;\mathrm{min}\) . After that, the above mixture was transferred to a \(50\;\mathrm{mL}\) flask and treated at \(90\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under \(900\;\mathrm{rpm}\) rotation for \(^{5\mathrm{~\scriptsize~h~}}\) in the dark. Finally, the mixture was centrifuged \((10000~\mathrm{\rpm}\) , \(10\;\mathrm{\min}\) ) and washed with ethanol to obtain PCN224 NPs. The synthesized PCN-224 NPs were redispersed in ethanol for further use.
## 2.3. Synthesis of PCN-224/Pt
In a typical synthesis, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}\) (0.01 M, \(0.1{-}10\,\upmu\mathrm{mol})\) was added into \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) deionized (DI) water containing PCN-224 NPs \(\left(20~\mathrm{mg}\right)\) at the mass ratios \(\mathrm{(H_{2}P t C l_{6}}\) : PCN-224) of 0.01:5, 0.2:5, and 1:5, respectively, and drastically stirred for \(^{1\mathrm{~h~}}\) . Subsequently, \(2~\mathrm{mL}\) \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) \((2\mathrm{~mg~mL^{-1}})\) ) was added into the solution and vigorously stirred for an additional \(^{3\mathrm{~h~}}\) . Finally, the mixture was collected by centrifugation at \(10000~\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(15~\mathrm{min}\) and washed with DI water three times to obtain PCN-224/Pt NPs.
Phosphatidylcholine (lipids) was employed to improve the hydrophilicity of PCN-224/Pt NPs. Specifically, PCN-224/Pt \(\left(10~\mathrm{mg}\right)\) and lipids \(\left(5~\mathrm{mg}\right)\) were mixed in \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) DI water and stirred overnight. The lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs were collected by centrifugation at \(10000\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(15~\mathrm{min}\) and washed with DI water three times to remove excessive lipids. The lipids-coated PCN-224 NPs without Pt decoration were applied as control and synthesized under identical conditions in the absence of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}\) .
## 2.4. Characterization
2.4. CharacterizationThe PCN-224/Pt NPs were characterized by field emission-scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM, Hitachi S-4800), transmission electron microscopy (TEM, FEI Talos F200S), UV--vis-NIR absorption spectrophotometer (Shimadzu UV-3600), Fourier transform infrared spectrometer (FT-IR, Nicolet iS50), and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Escalab 250Xi). The concentrations of PCN-224/Pt dispersions were determined by an inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES, Prodigy).
## 2.5. DOX-loading and releasing
2.5. DOX-loading and releasingDOX \((7~\mathrm{mg})\) was added into \(10\,\mathrm{\mL}\) PBS solution containing lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs \(\mathrm{[1~mg~mL^{-1}})\) under magnetic stirring for \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\) in the dark. The resulting dispersion was centrifuged ( \(13000\ \mathrm{rpm}\) , \(20\ \mathrm{min}\) ) and washed with PBS three times to obtain DOX-loaded PCN-224/Pt precipitate \((\mathrm{DOX@PCN-224/Pt})\) . The DOX content in the precipitate was assessed based on the standard absorbance-concentration curve (Fig. S1, supporting information). The entrapment efficiency (EE w/w%) and loading content (LC, w/ w%) of DOX were calculated from the equations: \(\mathrm{EE}_{\mathrm{DOX}}\,=\) (DOXloading weight/initial weight of DOX) \(\times~100\%\) , \(\mathrm{LC}_{\mathrm{DOX}}\,=\) (DOXloading weight/weight of DOX@PCN-224/Pt) \(\times~100\%\) .For the DOX-releasing, the DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs in a PBS solution ( \(10\,\mathrm{mL}\) , \(\mathrm{pH}=7.4\\) ) or an acid buffer ( \(10\,\mathrm{mL}\) , \(\mathrm{pH}=5.6\\) ) at the concentration of \(0.27\,\mathrm{~mg~}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) was gently stirred in the dark. At timepoints of \(0.5{-}12\,\mathrm{~h~}\) , a \(0.5\,\mathrm{\mL}\) dispersion was taken out and immediately centrifuged ( \(12000\ \mathrm{rpm}\) , \(15~\mathrm{min}\) ) and they were not returned to the original dispersion. Therefore, the concentration of PCN-224/Pt and the concentration of DOX (released or not released DOX) in the reaction solution remain unchanged. The DOX content in the supernatant was measured by using a UV– Vis spectrophotometer, and the time-dependent release rate was calculated by the equation: Release rate of \(\mathrm{DOX}~=\) (release weight/load weight) \(\times~100\%\) , where the release weight denoted the DOX content in supernatant and the load weight was the amount of whole DOX in \(0.5~\mathrm{mL}\) solution.
For the DOX-releasing, the DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs in a PBS solution ( \(10\,\mathrm{mL}\) , \(\mathrm{pH}=7.4\\) ) or an acid buffer ( \(10\,\mathrm{mL}\) , \(\mathrm{pH}=5.6\\) ) at the concentration of \(0.27\,\mathrm{~mg~}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) was gently stirred in the dark. At timepoints of \(0.5{-}12\,\mathrm{~h~}\) , a \(0.5\,\mathrm{\mL}\) dispersion was taken out and immediately centrifuged ( \(12000\ \mathrm{rpm}\) , \(15~\mathrm{min}\) ) and they were not returned to the original dispersion. Therefore, the concentration of PCN-224/Pt and the concentration of DOX (released or not released DOX) in the reaction solution remain unchanged. The DOX content in the supernatant was measured by using a UV– Vis spectrophotometer, and the time-dependent release rate was calculated by the equation: Release rate of \(\mathrm{DOX}~=\) (release weight/load weight) \(\times~100\%\) , where the release weight denoted the DOX content in supernatant and the load weight was the amount of whole DOX in \(0.5~\mathrm{mL}\) solution.
