Intracellular \(^10_{2}\) generation: CT26 cells were cultured with the new medium containing modified PCN-224/Pt \((100\,\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) in \(5\%\) \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) at \(37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(4\;\mathrm{h}\) . Then cells were washed with PBS, and incubated with \(100~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) serum-free medium containing DCFH-DA \((20\,\upmu\mathrm{M})\) . After incubation for \(^{1\mathrm{~h~}}\) , cells were irradiated with ultrasound \((1.0\,\mathrm{MHz},1.75\,\mathrm{W\,cm}^{-2})\) for \(5\,\mathrm{{min}}\) . Eventually, after washing the cells with PBS, the cells were observed using a digital microscope. For comparison, cells treated with lipids-coated PCN-224 \((100~\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) were also stained with DCFH-DA.
In vitro sonodynamic therapy: For SDT, CT26 cells were respectively incubated with DMEM containing lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt or PCN-224 \((100~\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) for \(12~\mathrm{h}\) . Then, cells were exposed to US irradiation \((1.0\,\mathrm{\:MHz},\,\,1.75\,\mathrm{\:W\,\cm}^{-2})\) for different times \(\left(0-\right.\) \(5\ \mathrm{min}\) ). After treatments, the cell viability was assessed by using the standard CCK-8 assay. In addition, the treated cells were stained with Calcein-AM and PI for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) and then observed by the fluorescence microscope.
In vitro DOX release: CT26 cells were seeded in 12-well plates \((1\,\times\,10^{5}\) cells/well) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . Afterward, the medium was replaced with a new medium containing DOX@PCN-224/Pt \((100~\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) . After incubation of 0.5, 1, or \(^{3\mathrm{~h~}}\) , the CT26 cells were washed with PBS to remove residual nanoparticles and stained with DAPI for an additional \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) , respectively. Finally, the cells were imaged by the fluorescence microscope.
## 2.9. Mice experiments
Tumor model: All animal investigations performed in accordance with the protocols approved by the Animal Welfare and Research Ethics Committee of Donghua University.
In vivo antitumor efficacy: Mice with CT26 tumors were randomly divided into four groups (four mice/group): (I) saline, (II) DOX@PCN-224/Pt, (III) lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt + US, (IV) DOX@PCN-224/Pt + US respectively. Tumor-bearing mice were intratumorally injected with saline solution \((150~\upmu\mathrm{L},\) Group I) or lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt \((150~\upmu\mathrm{L},~100~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) , group III) or DOX@PCN-224/Pt \((150~\upmu\mathrm{L},~100~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) , Group II and IV) saline dispersion. After injection, the tumors in groups III and IV were exposed to US irradiation \((1.75\,\mathrm{~W~}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) , \(10\,\mathrm{\min}\) ). Body weights and tumor sizes of mice were recorded every two days. The tumor sizes were calculated by using the formula: volume \(=\) length \(\times\) wi \(\mathrm{d}\mathrm{t}\mathrm{h}^{2}/2\) . Mice were sacrificed to gather tumors and major organs after 15 days. All tissues were sectioned for further H&E staining.
In vivo hypoxia alleviation efficiency: The hypoxia amelioration efficiency of tumors was studied by a HIF- \(1\alpha\) immunofluorescence staining. After receiving treatments, mice in group I and IV were sacrificed. The tumor tissues were prefixed with \(4\%\) paraformaldehyde, cut into slices, and then stained with a mouse anti-HIF- \(1\alpha\) monoclonal primary antibody and Alex488-conjugated goat antirabbit IgG secondary antibody under the guidance of manufacturers' introduction. Afterward, the immunofluorescence images were taken by using a fluorescence microscope.
