## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Synthesis and characterizations
The DOX@PCN-224/Pt nanocomposites were prepared by initially synthesizing PCN-224 NPs through the assembly of zirconium clusters and TCPP, then decorating Pt nanozymes and lipids on PCN-224 NPs, and finally loading DOX into the lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs, as shown in Fig. 1a. During the solvothermal process, the carboxyl groups of TCPP can coordinate with zirconium clusters to form the self-assembly of PCN-224 [44]. Subsequently, the Pt nanoclusters were decorated on PCN-224 to form PCN-224/Pt NPs. Notably, the PCN-224 NPs have a role in hindering the Pt agglomeration, which ensures the high catalase-like activity of Pt nanoclusters. To improve the biocompatibility, phosphatidylcholine was used to coat on the surface of PCN-224/Pt to make it highly stable in PBS solution. Finally, the antitumor chemotherapeutic drug DOX was loaded to form DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs. With US irradiation, the DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs exhibit high catalase-like activity, enhanced \(^{1}0_{2}\) generation ability, and DOX release capacity, which can work as a efficient nanoplatform for combined SDT-chemotherapy (Fig. 1b).
The morphology of PCN-224 and PCN-224/Pt NPs were firstly characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM). TEM and SEM images show that the PCN-224 NPs have spherical morphology with an average size of \(100\;\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 2a and Fig. 5a, supporting information). After the decoration of Pt, the PCN-224/Pt NPs exhibit similar spherical morphology, and there are well-dispersive small Pt NPs in/on PCN-224 NPs (Fig. 2a-b and Fig. S2b, supporting information). In addition, by adjusting the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224\) mass ratios (0.01:5, 0.2:5, 1:5), the structure of PCN-224/Pt NPs was further investigated. When the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224\) mass ratio is 0.01:5, the average size and number of Pt on PCN-224 NPs are \({\sim}1\;\mathrm{nm}\) and \({\sim}30\) , respectively (Fig. 2c-d). With the increase of the ratio from 0.01:5 to 0.2:5 and 1:5, the particle size of Pt increases to 1.5 and \(2.2\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 2c), while the average number of Pt increases to 106 and 153 (Fig. 2d). However, PCN-224 NPs prepared in the 1:5 mass ratio exhibit the corroded and linked shape (Fig. 2a-b), which likely emanate from the high content of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}\) . Considering the stability of PCN-224 NPs and the influence of Pt content on catalytic activity, the mass ratio of 0.2:5 is selected for the subsequent study. As revealed in high-resolution TEM images (Fig. 2e), PCN-224/Pt NPs have a distinct lattice spacing of \({\sim}0.339\ \mathrm{nm}\) , which is corresponding to the (1 1 1) plane of Pt crystal [45]. Therefore, the size and the number of Pt on PCN-224 can be well controlled by adjusting the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}/\mathrm{PCN}{-}224\) mass ratios.
