The elemental mapping images and energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) pattern of PCN-224/Pt NPs (Fig. 3a-b) indicate the homogeneous distribution of C, O, Zr, Pt signals, which verify the successful growth of Pt on PCN-224 NPs. Besides, X-ray photon spectroscopy (XPS) was employed to analyze the chemical valence of PCN-224/Pt NPs (Fig. 3c-d and Fig. S3, supporting information). In Zr 3d spectrum (Fig. 3c), the peaks at \(185.2\,\mathrm{eV}\) and \(182.8\,\mathrm{eV}\) correspond to \(3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}\) and \(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}\) of Zr (IV) states, respectively [46]. In Pt 4f spectrum (Fig. 3d), the peaks at \(75.2\,\mathrm{eV}\) and \(71.8\,\mathrm{eV}\) indicate the presence of Pt \(4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}\) and Pt \(4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}\) , which can be attributed to two states including \(\mathrm{Pt}^{0}\) ( \(75.2\ \mathrm{eV}\) , \(71.8\ \mathrm{eV})\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}^{2+}\) ( \(76.6\ \mathrm{eV}\) , \(73.0\ \mathrm{eV})\) , indicating that the well formation of zero-valent Pt nanoclusters. It is worth noting that the presence of divalent Pt might be due to the oxidation that occurs during sample transfer to the XPS chamber, resulting in the valence state fluctuation [47]. In addition,
the content of Pt element in PCN-224/Pt NPs was determined to be \( \sim17.49\,\mathrm{wt}\% \) by using inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry. In conclusion, all these results confirm the successful synthesis of PCN-224/Pt NPs.
To improve the hydrophilicity and biocompatibility, lipid molecules were coated on the surface of PCN-224/Pt. TEM image (Fig. S4a, supporting information) of lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs exhibits a similar spherical morphology. The dynamic average size of lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs increases to \( \sim198~\mathrm{nm} \) compared to that ( \( \sim143 \) and \( 154\;\mathrm{nm} \) ) of uncoated PCN-224 and PCN-224/Pt NPs (Fig. 4a). In addition, the zeta potential decreases from \( 8.8\,\mathrm{\mV} \) for un-coated PCN-224 NPs to \( -10.6\,\mathrm{\mV} \) for un-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs and \( -14.5\,\mathrm{\mV} \) for lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs (Fig. 4b). Both size and zeta potential evolutions suggest successful modification. As a result, un-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs are easily precipitated within 1 day (Fig. S5, supporting information), while lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs remain stable (Fig. S6, supporting information) during 6 days, indicating high dispersity and stability.
Subsequently, the photoabsorption and fluorescence of TCPP ligands, PCN-224 and PCN-224/Pt NPs were investigated (Fig. 4c). UV–vis absorption spectrum reveals that TCPP exhibits a major Soret peak at \( 418\,\mathrm{\nm} \) and four Q-bands at 514, 549, 590, and \( 646\,\mathrm{\nm} \). Compared to TCPP, both PCN-224 and PCN-224/Pt NPs have a major Soret peak at \( 422\ \mathrm{nm} \) and four Q-bands with slight red-shift, probably owing to the coordination interaction between TCPP and active nodes of \( Z\mathbf{r}_{6} \). Fluorescence spectrum indicates that TCPP has a strong fluorescence peak at \( 650\;\mathrm{nm} \) under \( 418\;\mathrm{nm} \) light
excitation (Fig. 4d). However, the assembled PCN-224 and PCN-224/Pt NPs display a weak fluorescence peak at \( 655~\mathrm{nm} \), due to the energy transfer from porphyrin ring to metal ions [8].
## 3.2. Detection of the enzyme-like activities and \( ^{1}O_{2} \)
Hypoxia and excessive hydrogen peroxide \( \left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right) \) are unique characteristics of the tumor microenvironment. \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) in hypoxia tumors can be depleted by catalase or catalase-like and converted to oxygen, which can enhance the production of \( ^{1}O_{2} \). Thus, we firstly investigated the catalase activity of PCN-224/Pt NPs by determining the concentration of dissolved oxygen. For \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) solution alone, the concentration of dissolved oxygen remains almost constant. Moreover, PCN-224 NPs solution containing \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) \( (250~\upmu\mathrm{M}) \) exhibits negligible \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) depletion (Fig. 5a). In contrast, when \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) was mixed with PCN-224/Pt solution \( (50~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}) \) at \( 37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), about \( 3.57\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1} \) of \( O_{2} \) was generated after 6 min accompanied by noticeable oxygen bubbles. Demonstrating the effective catalase-like activity of PCN-224/Pt NPs. Importantly, PCN-224/Pt NPs remain excellent catalytic performance and conversion from \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) to \( O_{2} \), under three cycles addition of \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) (Fig. 5b), revealing the catalytic stability of PCN-224/Pt NPs. The generated \( O_{2} \) could further promote the generation of \( ^{1}O_{2} \) under the irradiation of US. To verify the enhanced \( ^{1}O_{2} \) generation ability of PCN-224/Pt NPs under US irradiation, we used \( ^{1}O_{2} \) detection probe 1,3-diphenylisobenzofuran (DPBF), which DPBF can be oxidized in the presence of \( ^{1}O_{2} \), resulting in the irreversible reduction of char
acteristic absorption of DPBF at \(422~\mathrm{nm}\). When DPBF solution was irradiated with US alone \((1.75\,\textrm{W c m}^{-2})\), the intensity peaks of DPBF at \(422~\mathrm{nm}\) remain almost unchanged within \(10\;\mathrm{min}\), indicating that US alone has no impact on DPBF (Fig. S7a, supporting information). The DPBF solution containing PCN-224 NPs was treated by US irradiation, and the absorbance intensity at \(422\,\textrm{n m}\) decreased slightly with the increase of exposure time (Fig. S7b-c, supporting information), showing unsatisfactory \({}^{1}0_{2}\) production capacity regardless of whether \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) was added. On the contrary, the mixture of PCN-224/Pt NPs and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) has an enhanced \({}^{1}0_{2}\) generation capacity (Fig. 5c). As shown in Fig. 5d, for DPBF solution treated with PCN-224/Pt NPs \(+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{US}\), \(51.9\%\) of DPBF is oxidized within \(10\;\mathrm{min}\), which was significantly higher than the other three groups. Considering the excessive \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in hypoxia tumors, therefore, the above results suggest that PCN-224/Pt NPs might exhibit a remarkable SDT effect.
## 3.3. Cytotoxicity and SDT in vitro
Based on the excellent \(0_{2}\) and \({}^{1}0_{2}\) generation efficiency of PCN-224/Pt NPs, we further evaluated the therapeutic effect at the cellular level. Firstly, to assess whether lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs have high biological safety in biomedical applications, the cytotoxicity of lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs was evaluated by using a
standard cell counting kit-8 (CCK-8) assay with colon cancer cells (CT26), human ovarian cancer cells (SKOV3) and normal human umbilical vein epithelial cells (HUVEC). After the culture with lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt \((0{-}300~\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) for \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\), both CT26 and SKOV3 cancer cells show high viabilities. For example, when the concentration of lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs reaches \(300~\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\), the cell viabilities are \(81.2\%\) and \(75.6\%\), respectively (Fig. 6a and Fig. S8, supporting information). Obviously, no significant difference is observed in their viabilities between \(48\,\mathrm{~h~}\) and \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\) incubation, indicating the low cytotoxicity of lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs. Similarly, the cell viabilities of normal cells (HUVEC) are still higher than \(85\%\) after \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\) incubation with lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs (even at the concentration of \(300~\upmu\mathrm{g}\,\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\)) (Fig. 6b). Thus, the lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs exhibit higher cytotoxicity to cancer cells, which should be attributed to the overproduction of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in cancer cells. The Pt nanozymes in lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt can catalyze \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to convert \(0_{2}\) and enhance the nutrient blocking effect of cancer cells [48].
Subsequently, the intracellular \({}^{1}0_{2}\) production ability of lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs was evaluated by using \(2^{\prime}{,}7^{\prime}\)-dichlorodihydrofluorescein diacetate (DCFH-DA), which can be oxidized by intracellular \({}^{1}0_{2}\) to \(2^{\prime}{,}7^{\prime}\)-dichlorofluorescein (DCF) with green fluorescence [49]. For comparison, lipids-coated PCN-224 was prepared through the coordination interaction. Apparently, the
fluorescence images of cells treated with US, lipids-coated PCN-224 or lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt alone show the extremely weak green fluorescence (Fig. 6c and Fig. S9, supporting information). When cells are treated with lipids-coated PCN-224 and US irradiation, an apparent green fluorescence of DCF is observed (Fig. 6c), suggesting \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) generation inside cells. However, cells treated with lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs exhibit much stronger fluorescence intensity in comparison with the group of the lipids-coated PCN-224 NPs upon US. The intensity enhancement is due to the fact the Pt modification could catalyze intracellular \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to generate \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), which further enhances the \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) generation of lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs in the presence of US. Taken together, we can conclude that lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs have low cytotoxicity and can produce more \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) within cancer cells upon US irradiation.
Encouraged by the excellent intracellular \({}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) generation ability, the in vitro SDT performance of lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs was further investigated by the CCK-8 assay. CT26 cells were cultured with lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt or PCN-224 NPs \((100\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) for \(4\,\mathrm{h}\), followed by \(1.75\ \mathrm{W\ cm}^{-2}\) of US irradiation for different times \((0{-}5\ \mathrm{min})\). It was shown that the cell viability in the lipids-coated PCN-224 NPs group has a rapid decline from \(98\%\) to \(52.9\%\) with the increased irradiation time from 0 to \(5\ \mathrm{min}\) (Fig. 7a), while the cells treated with US irradiation alone still exhibit high viability. However, due to the effective conversion of intracellular \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) into \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) catalyzed by Pt, lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs exhibit significantly
higher SDT toxicity than lipids-coated PCN-224 NPs at tested irradiation time. The cell viabilities dramatically decrease from \(102\%\) at \(0\ \mathrm{min}\) to \(30.5\%\) at \(5\ \mathrm{min}\) of irradiation. It is worth noting that, when cells were irradiated by US for \(<3\ \mathrm{min}\), no significant difference is observed in cell viability between lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt group and lipids-coated PCN-224 group, which is probably attributed to the sufficient intracellular oxygen condition within \(3\ \mathrm{min}\). With the US irradiation time lasting for more than \(3\ \mathrm{min}\), the intracellular \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in both lipids-coated PCN-224 NPs and lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt groups is depleted rapidly, and the SDT toxicity effect of lipids-coated PCN-224 is largely inhibited by the insufficient oxygen concentration. However, in the lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt group, \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) can be supplemented with the assistance of Pt catalyzing intracellular \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), thus cells treated with lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt exhibit significant lower viability in comparison with that of the lipids-coated PCN-224 group. To proof the above hypothesis, an oxygen indicator \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{dpp})_{3}]^{2+}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) [49] was employed to investigate the intracellular oxygen level. The red fluorescence of \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{dpp})_{3}]^{2+}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) at \(613\ \mathrm{nm}\) was observed with a fluorescence microscope, which could be forcefully quenched by \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), suggesting the change of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) content. After incubation with DMEM containing lipid-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs, the red fluorescence of \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{dpp})_{3}]^{2+}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) within CT26 cells is nearly extinguished, showing greatly improved \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) level (Fig. 7b). Comparatively, the red fluorescence is maintained in control and lipids-coated PCN-224 groups.
Therefore, the promising reoxygenation capacity of the lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs could distinctly improve the oxygen deficiency of tumor cells.
In addition, the therapeutic effect of lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs was also confirmed by a live/dead cell staining assay. After different treatments, CT26 cells were co-stained with Calcein-AM and PI to distinguish alive (green color) and dead (red color) cells. As shown in fluorescence images (Fig. 7c), the cells exhibit green fluorescence only upon US irradiation in the absence of nano agent, demonstrating the safety of US. In addition, lipids-coated PCN-224 or lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt NPs alone induce negligible red fluorescence without US irradiation (Fig. S10, supporting information). On the contrary, the range of red fluorescence in lipids-coated PCN-224 + US and lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt + US group increases with the irradiation time \((0{-}3\ \mathrm{min})\). When cells are treated with US irradiation for \(5\ \mathrm{min}\), the red fluorescence appeared overwhelmingly in the group of lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt compared with lipids-coated PCN-224 (Fig. 7c). There is a significant difference in the number of dead cells between lipids-coated PCN-224 and lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt, which is consistent with the CCK-8 assay results, suggesting that lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt has an enhanced SDT efficacy.
## 3.4. DOX-releasing and SDT-chemo therapy in vitro
With outstanding specific surface area and porous structure, the obtained PCN-224/Pt NPs can serve as an anticancer drug delivery nanoplatform to facilitate combined SDT-chemo therapy. Thus, PCN-224/Pt was used to load the first-line chemotherapy doxorubicin (DOX), forming DOX@PCN-224/Pt. The presence of DOX in DOX@PCN-224/Pt can be confirmed by FTIR spectrum (Fig. S11, supporting information). UV-vis absorption spectra (Fig. S12, supporting information) reveal that DOX solution has the maximum absorption wavelength at \(490\ \mathrm{nm}\), and the absorbance at \(490\ \mathrm{nm}\) of DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs is higher than that of PCN-224/Pt NPs, indicating the successful loading of DOX. The loading content of DOX is \(26.3\%\) (263 \(\upmu\)g DOX in 1 mg DOX@PCN-224/Pt), which is similar to other MOFs carriers [8,50,51]. Then, the drug release
behavior of DOX@PCN-224/Pt was investigated in phosphate buffer saline (PBS, \(\mathrm{pH}=7.4\)) and acid buffer solution \((\mathrm{pH}=5.6)\), respectively. After \(12\ \mathrm{h}\), only about \(21.8\%\) of DOX is released from DOX@PCN-224/Pt in PBS (Fig. 8a). In comparison, DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs exhibit a rapid release behavior in acid buffer solution, where the DOX releasing amount is \(39.4\%\) at \(2\ \mathrm{h}\) and finally reaches up to \(43.2\%\) at \(12\ \mathrm{h}\), which is \(\sim1.98\) times higher than that \((21.8\%)\) in PBS. The enhancement is mainly ascribed to the change of the electrostatic attraction between PCN-224/Pt and DOX under neutral and acidic conditions [8]. These results suggest that DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs have good DOX loading ability and pH-dependent drug release behavior. Next, the in vitro drug release behavior was investigated by fluorescence microscopy. CT26 cells were incubated with DMEM containing DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs \((100\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1})\). After \(0.5\ \mathrm{h}\) incubation, a weak red fluorescence is observed (Fig. 8b), which is attributed to the fluorescence signal of DOX, indicating the cellular uptake of DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs. With the increase in incubation time, a significant red fluorescence is observed in CT26 cells, indicating the enhanced DOX release.
Furthermore, to investigate the therapeutic effects, the cell viabilities of chemotherapy (DOX@PCN-224/Pt group), SDT (PCN-224/Pt + US group), and combination therapy (DOX@PCN-224/Pt + US group) were assessed by utilizing the CCK-8 assay. When CT26 cells are treated with DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs without US irradiation (Fig. 8c), the cell viability decreases to \(63.5\%\), indicating the effective chemotherapy cytotoxicity of DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs. When CT26 cells are treated with PCN-224/Pt NPs + US (Fig. 7a), the cell viability decreases to \(30.5\%\) after \(5\ \mathrm{min}\). Importantly, the cell viability in DOX@PCN-224/Pt + US group decreases to \(36.3\%\) at \(3\ \mathrm{min}\) and \(8.37\%\) at \(5\ \mathrm{min}\), indicating that the combination of chemotherapy and SDT of DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs is superior to chemotherapy or SDT alone. Based on these results, DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs can achieve a high tumor therapeutical efficiency under US irradiation.
## 3.5. SDT-chemo therapy in vivo
Encouraged by the high in vitro SDT-chemotherapy efficiencies of DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs, we further assessed the in vivo antitumor
