To understand the mechanism of enhanced antitumor efficacy of \(\scriptstyle\mathrm{DOX}@\mathrm{PCN}-224/\mathrm{Pt}\) NPs, the hypoxia-inducible factor 1 (HIF-1α) expression was assessed by immunofluorescence staining, which is the major transcriptional regulator of cellular hypoxia [42]. It is shown that tumors in the control group have high expression of HIF-1α (Fig. 9e). By comparison, the green fluorescence is largely quenched in tumors after treatment with \(\scriptstyle\mathrm{DOX}@\mathrm{PCN}-224/\mathrm{Pt}\) NPs and US irradiation, indicating the reduction of HIF-1α, which possibly should stem from the sufficient oxygen supply by catalyzing intracellular \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) . These results indicate that \(\scriptstyle\mathrm{DOX}@\mathrm{PCN}-224/\mathrm{Pt}\) NPs could down-regulate the expression of HIF-1α and alleviate the tumor hypoxia, thus, enhance the sensitivity of tumor cells to SDT and chemotherapy. In addition, the tumor slices were stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) staining assay (Fig. 9f). The histological images in the control group exhibit normal cell morphologies. On the contrary, tumor cells in \(\scriptstyle\mathrm{DOX}@\mathrm{PCN}-224/\mathrm{Pt}+\mathrm{US}\) group have the most irregular cell morphology compared with
DOX@PCN-224/Pt group and lipids-coated PCN-224/Pt+US group, further confirming the remarkable destruction ability from DOX and toxic \(^10_{2}\). At the same time, the main organs in the control group reveal standard structure and morphology (Fig. 10). Obviously, no apparent tissue abnormality is observed in the SDT-chemo therapy group (IV. DOX@PCN-224/Pt+US). Therefore, DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs have low in vivo toxicity and can efficiently restrain tumor growth through combined SDT-chemo therapy.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we have developed the DOX@PCN-224/Pt nanoplatform with enhanced SDT-chemo therapy for tumor. The DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs are constructed by the sequential synthesis of the PCN-224 NPs (100 nm) through a solvothermal route, the insitu reduction of Pt nanoclusters (\(\sim\!1.5\;\mathrm{nm}\)) in the porous structure, and then the loading of anticancer drug DOX with the loading efficacy of 26.3%. The presence of Pt nanozymes can efficiently catalyze \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) into \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), which can not only facilitate the generation of cytotoxic \(^10_{2}\) from PCN-224 under US irradiation, but also substantially down-regulate the HIF-1α expression to enhance the sensitivity of tumors to chemotherapy. Thus, DOX@PCN-224/Pt nanoplatform has an enhanced \(^10_{2}\) generation capacity compared with other reported sonosensitizers (such as Zn-TCPP [52], FAHMME [53]). The in vivo therapeutic efficacy evaluations prove that the DOX@PCN-224/Pt NPs exhibit a remarkable enhancement in tumor suppression compared to chemotherapy or SDT alone. Therefore, this nanozyme-enhanced tumor treatment strategy has been proven to be a strong tool to fight against cancer and provides a new perspective to broaden the applications of nanozymes.
